{
  "teamId": 109,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Arizona Diamondbacks opened their season with an immediate gut punch, surrendering a pair of devastating home runs in the first week that set an anxious tone for what was to come. The bullpen, anchored by Paul Sewald, showed flashes of brilliance but proved maddeningly inconsistent — capable of preserving leads one night and surrendering walk-off blasts the next. Arizona's lineup, led by Ketel Marte's power surge and Corbin Carroll's emerging star turn, could generate offense, but the pitching staff too often undid the work of the bats.\n\nThrough the spring and into early summer, the D-backs sputtered through a series of demoralizing defeats. Rallies were snuffed out by timely double plays, and the bullpen arms of Juan Morillo and Paul Sewald became synonymous with late-inning heartbreak. Marte himself delivered one of the season's most crushing blows — from the wrong end — when his 6th home run of the year came as an opponent's clip, a reminder that the middle of the Arizona lineup was being matched, and sometimes exceeded, by rival offenses.\n\nA brief resurgence in mid-summer offered hope. Defensive miscues by opponents gifted the D-backs some wins, and Paul Sewald strung together a pair of high-leverage saves that briefly had Chase Field buzzing. But August proved punishing: Ryan Waldschmidt's pair of home runs allowed were separated by just weeks, and together they cost Arizona nearly 1.6 win probability — the kind of damage that quietly buries a playoff race. The Diamondbacks entered September needing to win almost everything and managed only flickers of life.\n\nThe final weeks told the whole story. A September 14th Corbin Carroll home run — allowed, not hit — stung at -0.705 WPA, and a Nolan Arenado grand slam surrendered in the penultimate week extinguished any remaining mathematical hope. Arizona finished 86-76, a winning record that felt hollow. Good enough to contend, not good enough to survive. A team of tantalizing talent that too often let moments slip through its fingers.",
